/** @page debugging Debugging

    @tableofcontents

    @section debugging_intro Introduction

    %Qore programs can be debugged at the source level: a debugger sets breakpoints on statements, stops threads at
    them, at exceptions or on request, steps through the code, and shows and changes the values of variables and
    evaluates expressions with them.  Debugging works the same in every @ref execution_modes "execution mode": the
    AST and IR interpreters and JIT-compiled code report each statement to an attached debugger, and an AOT-compiled
    function runs its debug IR in the IR interpreter while a debugger is attached.

    The debugger is a part of the %Qore library, with the @ref Qore::DebugProgram "DebugProgram",
    @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl" and @ref Qore::Breakpoint "Breakpoint" classes, and the following tools
    are built on it:

    |!Tool|!Description
    |\c qdbg|A command-line debugger for a program that it runs; see @ref debugging_qdbg
    |\c qdbg-server|A debug server: runs a program and lets debugger clients connect to it over a websocket; see @ref debugging_remote
    |\c qdbg-remote|A command-line debugger for the programs of a debug server, such as \c qdbg-server or a Qorus server; see @ref debugging_remote
    |\c qdbg-vsc-adapter|The debug adapter of Visual Studio Code, which implements the Debug Adapter Protocol; see @ref debugging_vscode

    @section debugging_programs Programs That Can Be Debugged

    All code can be debugged unless it is parsed with @ref no-debugging "%no-debugging"
    (@ref Qore::PO_NO_DEBUGGING "PO_NO_DEBUGGING"); the debug hooks remain dormant until a debugger attaches to a
    program, so they cost nothing in normal execution.  Code that controls a debugger, with the
    @ref Qore::DebugProgram "DebugProgram" and @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl" classes, must be parsed with
    @ref allow-debugger "%allow-debugger" (@ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER "PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER"), as a debugger can read
    and change the data of the debugged code.

    The debuggers parse a script in the mode that the interpreter gives its file (see
    @ref Qore::Program::applySourceDefaults() "Program::applySourceDefaults()"): a file whose name ends in <tt>.q</tt> is
    parsed in the default mode, other files in @ref modern "%modern" mode, as \c qore runs them.

    @section debugging_features Debugging Features

    @subsection debugging_breakpoints Breakpoints

    A breakpoint is set on a statement, which is given by:
    - its line in a file or source label; a line in a statement of more than one line is the statement that contains
      it.  The file can also be given with another path than it was parsed with, such as an absolute path for a
      program started with a relative path: it is then matched as an absolute path, made absolute with the working
      directory that the file was parsed in (see
      @ref Qore::ProgramControl::findStatementId() "ProgramControl::findStatementId()")
    - its line and column, for a statement of a line with more than one statement, as in
      <tt>int x = f(1); int y = g(2);</tt>; the breakpoint is on the last statement of the line that starts at or
      before the column
    - a function, possibly with the types of its parameters to select one variant of an overloaded function (a
      function with one variant can be given by its name only); the breakpoint is on the start of the function

    A breakpoint can have:
    - a condition: an expression that is evaluated with the variables of the thread that hits the breakpoint; the
      thread stops only if it is true
    - a hit condition: the thread stops only if the number of hits of the breakpoint with its condition true meets
      it: \c N (the Nth hit), \c ==N, \c >N, \c >=N, \c <N, \c <=N or \c %N (every Nth hit)
    - a log message: the breakpoint is a logpoint, which writes its message with the values of the expressions in
      braces, as in <tt>i={i} total={total}</tt>, and does not stop the thread

    The thread that hits a breakpoint checks its condition, hit condition and log message itself before it stops, so
    it does not wait for a debugger client at each hit; all breakpoints of the statement that apply to the thread are
    checked.  A condition or an expression of a log message that cannot be parsed with the variables of the statement
    of the breakpoint, as with a syntax error or a variable that the statement cannot refer to, is refused when it is
    set (see @ref Qore::ProgramControl::getStatementIdInfo() "ProgramControl::getStatementIdInfo()"); a condition that
    raises an error when it is evaluated stops the thread, and the error is shown.  The breakpoints, with their
    conditions, are shared by all clients of a debugger.

    @subsection debugging_exceptions Exceptions

    A thread stops at an exception according to the exception filters of the debugger clients: all user exceptions,
    all system exceptions, exceptions that no try statement of debuggable code in the call stack catches (uncaught
    exceptions), and exceptions with given codes, such as \c FILE-OPEN-ERROR.  A client without filters stops at all exceptions; an exception that no client stops at
    does not stop the thread.

    The debugger is told of an exception (@ref Qore::DebugProgram::onException() "DebugProgram::onException()") for
    each statement that the exception leaves: the statement that raised it, each statement that contains that one,
    and the statement of each caller, until a try statement catches it; it can dismiss the exception there, and the
    code then continues after that statement.  The events are the same in every execution mode: code with a parse
    tree runs on the AST interpreter while a debugger is attached, its top-level code too.

    @anchor debugger_aot_exception_events
    These events differ in two kinds of code:
    - an AOT-compiled function, which runs its debug IR while a debugger is attached: only the statement that raised
      the exception and the statements of its callers get an event, not the statements that contain them (an if,
      loop or try statement of the function), and a debugger that dismisses an exception where the debug IR cannot
      continue after the statement, as with an exception of an operator, ends the function, which returns no value
    - a function that was already running when the debugger attached keeps running as it did, and its statements get
      no event until it returns

    An exception that matches a @ref common_pitfalls "common pitfall" of %Qore, such as a variable that was declared
    but not assigned or a call reference to a deleted object, is shown with a hint and corrective actions: by \c qdbg
    and \c qdbg-remote when the thread stops at it, and by the debug adapter in the details of the exception (see
    @ref Qore::RuntimeDiagnosticInfo "RuntimeDiagnosticInfo").

    @subsection debugging_stepping Stepping

    A stopped thread continues:
    - to the next statement (step), also into the functions that the statement calls
    - to the next statement of its function (step over)
    - until its function returns (step out); it stops at the closing brace of the function before it returns
    - until a given statement (run to)
    - into a chosen call of its statement (step into a target), as \c g() in <tt>f(g(1), h(2))</tt>; the calls are
      given by @ref Qore::ProgramControl::getStepInTargets() "ProgramControl::getStepInTargets()", also calls of
      methods that a subclass can override, of call references and of closures, and each call of a function that the
      statement calls more than once; the thread stops at the start of the called code, and at the next statement if
      the statement does not make the call
    - until its next breakpoint or exception (run)

    Each step stops once at the next statement.

    A running thread can be stopped (paused), and all threads of a program can stop together when one of them stops,
    and continue together.

    @subsection debugging_variables Variables and Expressions

    The local variables of each frame of the call stack of a stopped thread and the global variables of its program
    are shown with their values; lists, hashes and objects are shown as trees of their elements, keys and members,
    also private ones, which are read when they are shown.  A variable, or an element, key or member of it, can be
    set to the value of an expression; the value is assigned as an assignment in the code assigns it, so a variable
    with a type converts it or refuses it, and a read-only variable cannot be set.  A variable that was declared but not assigned, whose type requires a value, is
    shown as such with its declared type, as <tt>int count = NOTHING (declared but not assigned)</tt>.

    Expressions are evaluated with the variables of a frame and the top-level local variables of the program, which
    have global scope, with the values of the thread (see
    @ref Qore::ProgramControl::getTopLevelLocalVars() "ProgramControl::getTopLevelLocalVars()"), for watches, hovers and
    debug consoles of IDEs and the
    \c eval command of the command-line debuggers.  An expression can refer to all code of the program, its functions,
    classes, constants and other declarations, also those that are not public (see
    @ref Qore::ProgramControl::createEvalProgram() "ProgramControl::createEvalProgram()").  It is evaluated in a
    separate @ref Qore::Program "Program" with @ref Qore::PO_LOCKDOWN "PO_LOCKDOWN", which cannot access the file
    system, the network or processes; the functions and methods that it calls run in the debugged program with its
    permissions.  The local and global variables are copied into variables of the expression, so an assignment in an
    expression does not change a variable of the program, while the members of objects are changed in the objects.

    At the stop at the start of a program, before its first statement, the thread has not entered the code of the
    program: expressions are evaluated with its code and global variables, and there are no local variables.

    @section debugging_vscode Debugging in Visual Studio Code

    The Qore extension of Visual Studio Code debugs %Qore programs with \c qdbg-vsc-adapter, which the extension finds
    next to the \c qore executable of the \c qore.executable setting, unless the \c qore.debugAdapter setting gives
    its path.

    A \c launch configuration runs a program in the debugger:
    @code{.json}
{
    "type": "qore",
    "request": "launch",
    "name": "Debug the current file",
    "program": "${file}",
    "args": ["-v", "input.csv"],
    "stopOnEntry": false
}
    @endcode

    An \c attach configuration connects to a program of a debug server, such as \c qdbg-server or a Qorus server:
    @code{.json}
{
    "type": "qore",
    "request": "attach",
    "name": "Attach to the debug server",
    "connection": "ws://localhost:8001",
    "program": "myfile.q"
}
    @endcode

    |!Attribute|!Request|!Description
    |\c program|both|the program to run, or the name of the program of the debug server to attach to
    |\c args|launch|the arguments of the program
    |\c stopOnEntry|launch|stop at the first statement of the program; default \c false
    |\c stopAllThreads|both|all threads stop when one of them stops, and continue together; default \c true
    |\c define, \c parseOptions, \c timeZone|launch|parse defines, parse options and the time zone of the program
    |\c connection, \c headers, \c proxy, \c timeout, \c connTimeout, \c respTimeout, \c maxRedir|attach|the connection to the debug server; a token of the server is given in \c headers as <tt>[{"name": "Authorization", "value": "Bearer &lt;token&gt;"}]</tt>
    |\c logFilename, \c appendToLog, \c verbosity, \c fullException|both|the log of the adapter and the exception information that it gives

    The exception breakpoints of the adapter stop at user, system or uncaught exceptions, and with the
    "Exceptions by code" filter at the exceptions with the codes of its condition, as in
    <tt>FILE-OPEN-ERROR, SOCKET-CONNECT-ERROR</tt>.

    The adapter supports source, function and exception breakpoints with conditions, hit conditions and log messages,
    breakpoints on any statement of a line (column breakpoints), stepping, also into a chosen call of a statement
    (step-in targets), pausing, all threads stopping together, call stacks, variables as trees whose values can be set,
    and expressions for watches, hovers and the debug console, where a line whose first word is a \c qdbg command is
    processed as the command; the shortcuts and aliases of \c qdbg, such as \c n and \c backtrace, are not commands
    there, as they can be the names of variables, and a line that starts with \c $ is always an expression.

    Stopping a launched program ends it, also its threads that wait, as for a lock or for input: they end with a
    \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception; disconnecting from an attached program leaves it running.  Restarting ends a
    launched program and launches it again with the same breakpoints, and attaches an attached program again.  Integers
    can be shown in hexadecimal where the IDE offers it.  See @ref vscdebugadapterintro for details and limitations.

    @section debugging_qdbg Command-Line Debugging with qdbg

    \c qdbg runs a program in the debugger and stops it at its start:
    @code{.sh}
qdbg myfile.q -my -program params
    @endcode

    \c qdbg reads commands from its input; \c TAB completes commands, \c help lists them with what they do and a quick
    start, and <tt>help command</tt> shows the details of a command.  Commands can be shortened while they stay unique,
    as <tt>br cr 10</tt> for <tt>break create 10</tt>.  The time zone of the program is given with \c -z as a region
    name, such as \c Europe/Prague, or as a UTC offset, such as \c +01:00.  With input that is not
    a terminal, each command that resumes a thread returns when a thread pauses again or the thread ends, so a file of
    commands is processed at the stops that it is written for:
    @code{.sh}
qdbg myfile.q < commands.txt
    @endcode

    Files of commands have one command on each line; lines that start with \c # are comments.  \c qdbg and
    \c qdbg-remote run the commands of the init file, <tt>.qdbginit</tt> in the home folder, at their start, unless \c -n is
    given, or of the file of \c -i instead, and then the files of \c -x, and continue with the commands of their input;
    the \c script command runs a file of commands at the prompt.  A command of a file that resumes a thread waits until
    a thread pauses, and the first command that fails ends the file.  A file of commands in the current folder is not
    run unless it is given with \c -i or \c -x:
    @code{.sh}
# ~/.qdbginit
options stopall set 1
exception filter uncaught
    @endcode

    \c SIGINT or \c SIGTERM ends \c qdbg and the program that it debugs, with the exit code 128 plus the number of the
    signal; it ends \c qdbg-remote, and the programs of its debug server continue.

    \c qdbg tells what happens in plain words, such as <tt>Paused at breakpoint 1 at main.q:3 (thread 2)</tt>, followed
    by the line of code.  A thread that the program starts runs until it reaches a breakpoint, as without the debugger,
    and a thread that pauses while the current thread runs becomes the current thread; the end of a thread is shown,
    but it does not pause the thread.  The call stacks show the frames of the program, each named after its function,
    without the frames of the debugger.

    |!Command|!Description
    |<tt>continue</tt> (<tt>c</tt>), <tt>step</tt> (<tt>s</tt>), <tt>next</tt> (<tt>n</tt>), <tt>finish</tt>|shortcuts of <tt>go run</tt>, <tt>go step</tt>, <tt>go stepover</tt> and <tt>go untilreturn</tt>, as in other debuggers; \c finish pauses at the next statement of the caller and shows the value that the function returned
    |<tt>print expression</tt>, <tt>locals</tt>|shows the value of an expression, as \c eval, and the local variables of the current frame with their values
    |<tt>b location</tt>|adds a breakpoint, as <tt>break create</tt>
    |<tt>up [n]</tt>, <tt>down [n]</tt>|selects the frame that called the current frame, or the one that it called, and shows its line
    |<tt>kill</tt>|ends the current program: its threads end with a \c THREAD-CANCELLED exception, also those that wait
    |<tt>script file</tt>|runs the commands of a file
    |<tt>session save\|load file</tt>|saves the breakpoints, the display expressions, the exception filters and the \c stopall option to a file, or loads them, as with \c -s
    |<tt>break create [file] line[:column]</tt>|creates a breakpoint on a statement of a line; the column is counted in characters from 1
    |<tt>break create function[(types)]</tt>|creates a breakpoint on the start of a function
    |<tt>break list</tt>|lists the breakpoints of the current program with their conditions, hit conditions, log messages and number of hits
    |<tt>break condition\|hitcondition\|log id [value]</tt>|sets or removes the condition, hit condition or log message of a breakpoint
    |<tt>break enable\|disable\|delete id</tt>|enables, disables or deletes a breakpoint
    |<tt>exception filter [user] [system] [uncaught] [code ...] \| all \| none</tt>|shows or sets the exceptions that stop threads; a code, such as \c FILE-OPEN-ERROR, selects the exceptions with the code
    |<tt>stmt line [file] line</tt>|lists the statements of a line with their columns
    |<tt>go [run\|step\|stepover\|untilreturn] [statement]</tt>|resumes the current thread, optionally until a statement; \c go alone runs it until its next breakpoint or exception
    |<tt>thread targets</tt>, <tt>go stepinto [n]</tt>|lists the calls of the current statement, and steps into one of them; without \c n, it steps into the only call of the statement
    |<tt>bt</tt>, <tt>frame n</tt>|shows the call stack of the current thread with the lines and columns of its statements, and selects a frame
    |<tt>thread local [name [value]]</tt>|lists the local variables of the current frame, or shows or sets one
    |<tt>thread var path [from index \| = expression]</tt>|shows a variable or an element, key or member of it, as in <tt>h.lines[1]</tt> or <tt>h{key}</tt>, with its elements, keys and members, also from an index, or sets it to the value of an expression
    |<tt>display [expression]</tt>, <tt>undisplay [n]</tt>|adds an expression that is shown at each stop, or lists them, and removes one or all of them
    |<tt>eval expression</tt>|evaluates an expression with the variables of the current frame
    |<tt>thread list</tt>, <tt>bt all</tt>|lists the threads of the current program, whether they are paused, and where they are, and shows their call stacks
    |<tt>program stop [tid]</tt>|stops the threads of the program, or one of them
    |<tt>options stopall set 0\|1</tt>|all threads of a program stop together when one of them stops, and continue together
    |<tt>options waittimeout set ms</tt>|the time that a command that resumes a thread waits for a thread to pause, as with \c --wait-timeout

    See @ref debugcmdlineintro for all commands.

    @subsection debugging_qdbg_json JSON Output for Programs and Coding Agents

    With \c --json, \c qdbg and \c qdbg-remote write JSON Lines: one JSON object on each line, which a program, such
    as a coding agent, reads as it writes commands to their input.  Commands are read without a prompt, and an empty
    line is not a command.  The objects are:
    - \c result: the result of each command, with the \c command, \c ok, the text that the command printed in
      \c output, the \c error of a command that failed, \c timedOut if no thread paused in time (see below), and
      \c quit for a command that ends the debugger
    - \c event: an event of the debugger, with its name in \c event, its text, such as
      <tt>Paused at breakpoint 1 at main.q:3 (thread 2)</tt>, in \c text, and its \c data, such as the file, line and
      exception of a stop; the stop at an exception that matches a common pitfall gives its hint in \c hint
    - \c output: the output of the program, with its \c category (\c stdout or \c stderr) and \c text; the output
      of a program of \c qdbg is not written to the output of \c qdbg
    - \c end (\c qdbg): the end of the program, with the \c exception that ended it, or \c terminated for a program
      that the \c kill command ended
    - \c connected (\c qdbg-remote): the connection to the debug server
    - \c message and \c error: text that is printed outside of a command, and an error of the debug server

    With \c --wait-timeout, a command that resumes a thread waits for a thread to pause as many milliseconds at most;
    then the program is paused, and the command waits as long again for the pause, or as long as \c --pause-timeout
    says.  A thread that waits, as for a lock or I/O, pauses only when it continues; \c kill ends the program also then,
    and waits for its end as long as \c --wait-timeout says.  The wait of \c qdbg for the start of the script has no time
    limit: it ends when the script has attached or ended, or on \c SIGINT or \c SIGTERM, so the first command always has
    its program.
    @code{.sh}
printf 'break create 3\ngo\nlocals\ngo\nquit\n' | qdbg --json --wait-timeout=5000 myfile.q
    @endcode

    @section debugging_mcp Debugging with Coding Agents

    \c qdbg-mcp runs an <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io">MCP</a> server that lets coding agents, such as
    Claude Code, debug %Qore programs (see @ref debugmcpserverintro).  It listens on \c 127.0.0.1 and prints its URL;
    the agent connects to it with the HTTP transport of MCP:
    @code{.sh}
qdbg-mcp -l 127.0.0.1:8100
claude mcp add --transport http qore-debug http://127.0.0.1:8100/mcp
    @endcode

    The agent launches a script with \c qore_debug_launch, which runs in the process of the server and pauses at its
    start, or attaches to a debug server, such as \c qdbg-server or Qorus, with \c qore_debug_attach.  It sets
    breakpoints with conditions with \c qore_debug_set_breakpoint, and resumes threads with \c qore_debug_continue,
    which returns when a thread pauses, the program ends, or its timeout passes; then the program is paused, so that
    a loop that does not end can be inspected.  At a pause, \c qore_debug_stack, \c qore_debug_variables and
    \c qore_debug_evaluate read the program; \c qore_debug_command runs any command of \c qdbg.  Each tool reports
    the pauses with their locations and exceptions, the output of the program and its end since the last tool; an
    exception that matches a @ref common_pitfalls "common pitfall" is reported with a hint, and a variable that was
    declared but not assigned is marked as such.  A stop is given with its reason, such as \c breakpoint, \c step or
    \c exception; a small list, hash or object is given with its whole value, and a string as it is; the messages of
    logpoints are reported apart from the output of the program; and the end of a program reports the end of each of
    its threads.  A tool rejects an argument that it does not have, so that a misspelled option is not ignored.

    The tools can run code with the permissions of the server: a launched script, and expressions with the code of
    the debugged programs.  With \c -k, or the \c QDBG_MCP_TOKEN environment variable, the server requires a token,
    which the agent sends as a bearer token; it does not listen on an address that other hosts can reach without one,
    and \c --no-launch allows attaching to debug servers only.

    @section debugging_remote Remote Debugging

    \c qdbg-server runs a program and lets debugger clients connect to it over a websocket; it holds the program at
    its start until a client attaches, and it writes the output of the program to its terminal and sends it to the
    clients:
    @code{.sh}
qdbg-server -l 127.0.0.1:8001 myfile.q -my -program params
    @endcode

    Without \c -l, the server listens on a free port of \c 127.0.0.1, which it writes to its terminal, so that only
    local clients can connect; a listener given with \c -l and a port only listens on all interfaces.  As a client
    can evaluate expressions and change the program, a server that other hosts can reach should require a token: the
    server reads it from the file given with \c -k or from the \c QDBG_SERVER_TOKEN environment variable, and the
    clients send it as a bearer token in the \c Authorization header:
    @code{.sh}
QDBG_SERVER_TOKEN=s3cret qdbg-server -l 0.0.0.0:8001 myfile.q
qdbg-remote -H "Authorization=Bearer s3cret" myhost:8001
    @endcode

    \c qdbg-remote is the command-line client, with the commands of \c qdbg; it starts with the debugged program and
    its stopped thread as the current ones, and with input that is not a terminal it processes a file of commands at
    the stops that they are written for, as \c qdbg does:
    @code{.sh}
qdbg-remote -v localhost:8001
qdbg-remote localhost:8001 < commands.txt
    @endcode

    When the last client of a debug server disconnects, the stopped threads continue, and without a client a thread
    stops only at the start of a program, where the server holds it until a client connects.

    Visual Studio Code connects to a debug server with an \c attach configuration (see @ref debugging_vscode).  A
    Qorus server is a debug server for its interfaces, which run as programs of the server; \c qdbg-remote connects
    to it with a Qorus connection name, as in <tt>qdbg-remote qorus</tt>.

    The clients of a debug server share its breakpoints with their conditions, while each client selects the
    exceptions that stop threads for it.  The protocol is implemented by the
    @ref debugprogramcontrolintro "DebugProgramControl" and @ref debughandlerintro "DebugHandler" modules.

    @section debugging_api Writing a Debugger

    A debugger is a @ref Qore::DebugProgram "DebugProgram" subclass whose methods are called at the events of the
    threads of the programs that it debugs: at statements, function entries and exits, exceptions, and the start and
    end of threads; a method stops a thread by not returning until the thread continues.
    @ref Qore::ProgramControl "ProgramControl" gives the information of a debugged program: its statements, given by
    their location (@ref Qore::ProgramControl::findStatementId() "findStatementId()",
    @ref Qore::ProgramControl::findStatementIds() "findStatementIds()") and described with their columns
    (@ref Qore::ProgramControl::getStatementIdInfo() "getStatementIdInfo()"), the calls of a statement
    (@ref Qore::ProgramControl::getStepInTargets() "getStepInTargets()"), and a Program for evaluating expressions
    (@ref Qore::ProgramControl::createEvalProgram() "createEvalProgram()").  @ref Qore::Breakpoint "Breakpoint"
    objects are assigned to statements and stop the threads that reach them.

    The @ref debugprogramcontrolintro "DebugProgramControl" module implements a debugger with a protocol of commands
    and events for its clients, which @ref debugcmdlineintro "DebugCmdLine" and
    @ref vscdebugadapterintro "VscDebugAdapter" implement clients of.

    @section debugging_limitations Limitations

    - A breakpoint is on a statement, so an expression with more than one call, such as <tt>f(g(x))</tt>, has one
      breakpoint location.
    - Data breakpoints, which stop when a value changes
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5565">issue 5565</a>), jumping to a line and restarting
      a frame (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5566">issue 5566</a>) are not supported.
    - In AOT-compiled code, whose calls have no columns, stepping into a call matches the functions that the
      statement calls with the name of the call and the number of its calls before it; when an operand of a
      conditional operator, such as <tt>?:</tt>, <tt>&&</tt> or <tt>||</tt>, is not evaluated, a later call with the
      same name can be stepped into instead.
    - When a thread stops, the other threads stop at their next statement, so a thread that waits, as for input,
      stops when it runs again.
    - The columns of the debugger count bytes; the clients convert them to characters with the text of the line, and
      count a character as a byte for a source whose text is not available.
    - A function of AOT-compiled code that is already running when a debugger attaches, and a closure created in it,
      does not stop at breakpoints or steps, as it continues in its native code; functions that are called after the
      debugger attached are debugged
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5567">issue 5567</a>).
*/
